    { id: "doctor", kicker: "Doctor", heading: "Meet the specialist", blocks: [
      { kind: "doctor", name: "Dr Shahbaz Alam Khan", specialty: "General, GI & Laparoscopic Surgery • Hopewell Hospital, Ranchi", focus: "Areas of focus may include fissure, fistula, piles, gallbladder surgery, hernia and minimally invasive procedures." }
    ] },
    { id: "faq", kicker: "FAQs", heading: "Frequently asked questions", blocks: [
      { kind: "faq", items: [{ q: "Can an anal fissure heal without surgery?", a: "Yes. Many acute fissures heal with fibre, fluids, stool softening, warm sitz baths and topical medicines. Surgery is usually reserved for fissures that become chronic or fail conservative treatment." }, { q: "What is the difference between piles and fissure?", a: "A fissure is a tear in the anal lining and typically causes sharp pain during bowel movements. Piles are swollen haemorrhoidal tissue and more commonly cause bleeding, swelling or prolapse." }, { q: "Is Botox a permanent treatment?", a: "Botox temporarily relaxes the internal anal sphincter and can help a fissure heal. It does not work for everyone, and symptoms can recur after the effect wears off." }, { q: "When is surgery recommended?", a: "Surgery may be considered for chronic fissures that do not improve with appropriate medical treatment, especially when pain is persistent and examination confirms chronic disease." }, { q: "Will fissure surgery affect bowel control?", a: "Most patients do well, but sphincter surgery carries a small risk of continence disturbance. This risk is considered carefully before recommending surgery." }, { q: "Can fissure come back after treatment?", a: "Yes. Recurrence is possible, especially if constipation, hard stool or straining returns. Long-term bowel-habit correction remains important even after successful treatment." }] }
    ] }
